---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import ToolGallery from '../../components/ToolGallery.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is a seal bore liner packer used for?',
    a: 'A seal bore liner packer provides the pressure seal at the top of a liner, where the liner overlaps the casing string above it, by presenting a smooth, precision-finished seal bore that a tieback or production seal assembly stabs into and seals against from above. It completes the pressure-isolation job that the liner\'s hanging mechanism alone doesn\'t guarantee.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Isn\'t hanging the liner\'s weight the same thing as sealing it?',
    a: 'No — the two are related but separate functions handled by different parts of the overlap assembly. The liner\'s weight-bearing job is done by the hanger\'s slips, which anchor it mechanically to the casing above. The pressure-sealing job across the overlap is a separate function, provided by the seal bore liner packer (or in some designs, an integrated packer element within the hanger assembly itself).',
  },
  {
    q: 'What stabs into the seal bore?',
    a: 'A seal assembly or tieback stinger, run on the bottom of the string being connected above — whether that\'s a tieback string extending the liner\'s isolation up to surface, or a production string\'s own seal assembly — lands and seals inside the polished seal bore, completing the pressure barrier across the overlap.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What causes a seal bore liner packer to fail to seal?',
    a: 'Debris or cement left in the seal bore from the cementing operation can prevent the seal assembly from stabbing in cleanly or sealing fully once landed. Seal element wear on the stabbing assembly itself, rather than the bore, is another common cause, particularly after the seal assembly has been run and pulled more than once.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Seal Bore Liner Packer: Sealing the Liner-to-Casing Overlap"
  description="What a seal bore liner packer is, how it seals the liner-casing overlap separately from the hanger's weight-bearing job, what stabs into the seal bore, and common failure modes."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Equipment', href: '/equipment' }, { label: 'Seal Bore Liner Packer', href: '/equipment/seal-bore-liner-packer' }]}
  lang="en"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Equipment — Completion Tools</div>
    <h1>Seal Bore <span>Liner Packer</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Provides the pressure seal at the liner-to-casing overlap through a polished bore that a tieback or production seal assembly stabs into from above.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <ToolGallery image1="/images/equipment/seal-bore-liner-packer-1.jpg" image2="/images/equipment/seal-bore-liner-packer-2.jpg" alt="a generic seal bore liner packer" />
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="what-it-is">What a Seal Bore Liner Packer Is</h2>
        <p>A seal bore liner packer sits at the top of a <a href="/equipment/liner-hanger">liner</a>, where the liner overlaps the casing string above it, presenting a smooth, precision-finished internal bore. A separate seal assembly or tieback stinger, run on the string above, stabs into that polished bore and seals against it — completing the pressure barrier across the overlap interval.</p>

        <h2 id="hanging-vs-sealing">Hanging the Liner's Weight Is a Separate Job</h2>
        <p>It's easy to assume that hanging a liner and sealing it are the same operation, but they're handled separately within the overlap assembly. The liner's weight is carried by the <a href="/equipment/liner-hanger">liner hanger's</a> slips, which bite into the casing above and mechanically anchor the liner in place. The pressure seal across that same overlap is a distinct function — provided either by a dedicated seal bore liner packer like this one, or in some designs by an integrated packer element built into the hanger assembly itself.</p>

        <h2 id="what-stabs-in">What Stabs Into the Seal Bore</h2>
        <p>A seal assembly or tieback stinger — run on the bottom of whatever string is being connected from above, whether a tieback extending isolation up to surface or a production string's own seal assembly — lands inside the polished seal bore and seals against it, completing the pressure barrier. Because the seal bore is a fixed, precision surface, that stabbing connection can typically be made, pulled, and remade multiple times without disturbing the liner itself.</p>

        <h2 id="failure-modes">Common Failure Modes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Debris or cement residue</strong> left in the seal bore from the cementing operation, preventing a clean stab-in or a full seal once the seal assembly lands.</li>
          <li><strong>Seal element wear</strong> on the stabbing assembly itself, particularly after it has been run and pulled more than once over the life of the well.</li>
          <li><strong>Misalignment on stabbing</strong>, if the running string isn't properly centered relative to the seal bore during the connection attempt.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="vs-liner-hanger">Seal Bore Liner Packer vs. Liner Hanger</h2>
        <p>The two work together at the same location but do different jobs. The <a href="/equipment/liner-hanger">liner hanger</a> is what actually supports the liner's weight against the casing above. The seal bore liner packer is what actually stops pressure from communicating across the overlap. A completion can, in principle, hang a liner without a seal bore packer if isolation across the overlap isn't required — but any job that needs that overlap pressure-isolated needs this component specifically.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="toc">
          <div class="toc-title">On This Page</div>
          <a href="#what-it-is">What It Is</a>
          <a href="#hanging-vs-sealing">Hanging vs. Sealing</a>
          <a href="#what-stabs-in">What Stabs In</a>
          <a href="#failure-modes">Failure Modes</a>
          <a href="#vs-liner-hanger">vs. Liner Hanger</a>
          <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
        </div>
        <div class="note-box mt-24">
          <strong>Reference only.</strong> Seal bore dimensions and pressure ratings vary by manufacturer and model — always confirm against the actual tool's spec sheet before relying on any figure here for a real job.
        </div>
        <div class="card" style="margin-top:20px;border:1px solid var(--iron);border-radius:4px;">
          <h3>Related Reading</h3>
          <a href="/equipment/liner-hanger" class="card-link">→ Liner Hanger</a><br><br>
          <a href="/equipment/packer" class="card-link">→ Packer</a><br><br>
          <a href="/equipment/expandable-sand-screen" class="card-link">→ Expandable Sand Screen</a><br><br>
          <a href="/drilling/casing" class="card-link">→ Casing Design Explained</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
